        "markdown": "# 4 · Define the National-Interest Standard\n## 15:23–27:18\n\nLionel distinguishes personal preparedness from prayer, then argues that Iran is not capable of conventionally defeating or occupying the United States. He asks whether military escalation serves an American national interest and attributes U.S. involvement primarily to the alliance with Israel. \n\nHe rejects several ideological shorthand labels and repeatedly narrows his criterion to one question: **war**. Diplomatic or economic cooperation may be debated, but military involvement is where his standard changes. \n\n**Existing move:** `Lionel Defines an Evaluative Standard`.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786076913184,
          "reason": "node-batch-create"
        }
      }
    },
