      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "lionel-validation-study-09"
        },
        "markdown": "# 10 · Autobiographical Memory and Community Close\n## 1:11:19–1:17:42\n\nLionel explicitly says the Tyler Robinson and Candace stories are fascinating but rejects the idea that the show is a one-topic project. He broadens the show's identity around inquiry into larger issues. \n\nHe then describes an ordinary family scene at a farm, contrasts it emotionally with the prospect of war, recalls a Vietnam-era song about a father losing his son, remembers casualty reports from childhood, and says the present situation feels like the same question returning. \n\nThe episode ends by thanking the audience, praising their ability to follow nuanced subjects, promoting another broadcast, requesting likes and subscriptions, and using the recurring signoff. \n\n**Function:** transform policy analysis into autobiographical moral memory, then reaffirm the community capable of carrying that discussion.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786076913184,
          "reason": "node-batch-create"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "lionel-study09-grammar",
